I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern range, and the reusable refinement utilities in `learned/`. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible phase inventory, test alternates under a common profile model, and only export after replaying the final GSAS-II project.

The chemistry points first to lead tungstate, but Pb–W–O at 500 °C can retain PbO/WO₃ or access alternate PbWO₄ records. I’m treating those as an inventory to test rather than assuming a single product, and I’m now locating the local COD search/indexing and GSAS-II interfaces.

The local COD inventory contains two physically distinct PbWO₄ models: tetragonal scheelite/stolzite (I4₁/a) and monoclinic raspite (P2₁/a), plus the precursor polymorphs. I’m comparing their calculated reflection families against the raw peak positions before any scale or cell refinement; this is the key phase-count decision.

The unmatched reflections separate into two coherent families: the 23.36/23.88/24.65° triplet and related peaks match room-temperature WO₃, while 29.34/30.61/38.11/49.52/53.38/60.55/63.28° match orthorhombic massicot PbO. I’m now testing one-, two-, and three-phase inventories with identical background, angular, and profile treatment; raspite PbWO₄ and litharge PbO remain explicit challengers.

The common three-phase refinement gives a chemically coherent first estimate: about 76 wt% tetragonal PbWO₄, 13 wt% WO₃, and 11 wt% massicot PbO. The residual precursor mole amounts are nearly paired, as expected for incomplete PbO + WO₃ → PbWO₄ conversion. I’m re-challenging that inventory now with raspite, litharge, alternate COD records, and alternate WO₃ polymorphs; the provisional U term reached its nonnegative bound, so it will be pruned rather than used as evidence.

Two weak-phase challengers are nonzero and improve the fit: litharge PbO (~3 wt%) and raspite PbWO₄ (~6 wt%) when tested separately. Their strongest lines are partly overlapped, so neither is accepted yet. I’m testing them together, testing Pb₃O₄ (chemically plausible near 500 °C in air), and swapping the WO₃/primary PbWO₄ records under the same nuisance model to distinguish true families from intensity surrogates.

The challenger batch is still refining six replayable projects in parallel. The decision rule here is stricter than raw Rwp: a candidate must retain positive scale and explain multiple isolated or partly overlapped reflections after the common profile is rebuilt.

One result has resolved cleanly: the ε-WO₃ record is rejected—its scale collapses to the lower bound while the other phases absorb the pattern. The slower monoclinic/triclinic comparisons are still converging; I’m letting them finish because WO₃ cell setting and peak splitting materially affect the minor-phase fractions.

The challenge results now favor four phases: stolzite PbWO₄, triclinic/near-monoclinic WO₃, massicot PbO, and litharge PbO. Litharge is replay-stable at ~3.3 wt% and outperforms raspite or Pb₃O₄; raspite drops to ~1.1 wt% and worsens the combined model, so it remains rejected. I’m inspecting reflection-by-reflection residuals next, then I’ll test physically meaningful width terms one phase at a time.

The baseline tests support a smooth 10-term Chebyshev background and a refined polarization factor (~0.81); both improve coherent residual trends without invoking phase texture. SH/L asymmetry is not supported, and a W-only profile is clearly inadequate, so the clean common profile is fixed at U = V = 0 with refined W and X. I’m rebuilding that accepted baseline before testing phase size/strain.

The phase-width trials are separating cleanly: WO₃ isotropic size changes its scale noticeably, while WO₃ strain is effectively neutral; litharge strain increases its fraction, so I’m checking whether that gain is replay-stable or simply compensating its overlapped weak peaks. The remaining phase trials are still finishing.

The width audit rejects isotropic size/strain for stolzite and WO₃ (they return to neutral bounds). Massicot clearly needs a different line-shape description, but both isotropic size and strain run to extreme bounds and distort its fraction, so I am not accepting them. This triggers the requested return to inventory/baseline: I’m testing the alternate physical massicot record and symmetry-appropriate anisotropic broadening before any quantitative handoff.

The alternate massicot model is an incommensurate α-PbO approximant with a 44.86 Å supercell, so it is physically meaningful rather than an arbitrary database duplicate. Its many split reflections make the refinement slower; I’m retaining it long enough to see whether those splittings replace the bound broadening.

The incommensurate massicot supercell does not solve the problem: it gives a poor fit and drives litharge to zero, so the simpler Pbcm massicot record remains the physical baseline. The orthorhombic axis trials are now running; these test whether only selected hkl families are broadened, which is the appropriate alternative to the rejected isotropic extremes.

The completed axis trials show the same warning pattern for massicot: all axes seek extreme broadening and inflate its fraction, so anisotropy does not rescue a stable physical width model. Litharge’s strain response is modest and similar for all axes rather than axis-selective. One litharge size trial is still converging; the current evidence favors pruning all phase-specific broadening and reporting the resulting fraction uncertainty explicitly.

Lowering the crystallite-size bound resolved the massicot instability: all three axes converge to interior 7–21 nm domain sizes, and [100] is distinctly best and replay-stable. That model lowers Rwp from 15.62% to 14.62% but raises massicot to ~26 wt%, so I’m testing complementary strain and re-challenging litharge under this broadened massicot baseline before trusting the fraction.

The complementary tests are decisive: adding massicot strain forces both strain and one size component to bounds, so it is pruned. Litharge size/strain gives only small, non-axis-specific gains on heavily overlapped peaks and is also pruned. The accepted microstructure model is therefore only anisotropic massicot domain size ([100], ~8 and 21 nm). I’m now comparing no texture against four symmetry-distinct stolzite axes.

Texture is not accepted: all four stolzite axes give similarly small gains with March–Dollase factors close to 1, so the preferred axis is not uniquely supported by multiple families. I’m retaining the no-texture model and now performing the clean inventory re-challenge: first remove litharge, then add raspite and Pb₃O₄ separately while rebuilding background, scales, zero, cells, W/X, and massicot size.

The inventory re-challenge keeps litharge: removing it worsens Rwp from 14.62% to 15.01%. Raspite collapses to zero scale under the accepted broadened-massicot profile. Pb₃O₄, however, remains at 1.5 wt%, refines to a plausible cell, and lowers Rwp to 14.51%; its 26.8°, ~31°, 34.4°, and higher-angle families make it a credible fifth phase. I’m rebuilding once more with Pb₃O₄ and re-testing raspite plus the monoclinic WO₃ record.

The final-condition WO₃ comparison is effectively tied: triclinic gives Rwp = 14.5127%, while the room-temperature monoclinic P2₁/n record gives 14.5176%. Because the triclinic model spends extra cell degrees of freedom for only a 0.005-point gain, I’m checking parameter-count penalties and refined geometry; absent decisive splitting evidence, the physically matched room-temperature monoclinic model should be preferred.

The final model choice is now set: triclinic WO₃ is retained because it lowers χ² by ~96 with a more plausible Pb₃O₄ cell, though monoclinic WO₃ remains a near-degenerate symmetry alternative. The broadening model materially shifts PbWO₄/massicot fractions (roughly 73/10 wt% without it versus 61/25 wt% with it), so I will submit the better physical line-shape model but explicitly mark those fractions as model-dependent rather than overstate the formal errors.

The final replay converged with no bound/frozen variables (maximum shift/esd 0.66). It exports five CIFs and gives Rwp = 14.513%. I’m doing the final consistency audit now: CIF composition/occupancy/geometry, project-to-CIF cell agreement, normalized fractions, and pointwise `observed − calculated = difference`.

The audit found one structural export issue before handoff: the litharge CIF currently expands as Pb₂O, indicating an origin/space-group-setting mismatch inherited from the P4/nmm source record. I’m correcting that model and re-refining; the other four CIFs are full-occupancy, compositionally correct, and have plausible bond ranges.

The corrected litharge model is retained and materially improves the science: Rwp drops to 14.02%, the PbO stoichiometry is now correct, and the fractions become far more balanced (about 67.7% PbWO₄, 10.6% WO₃, 14.3% massicot, 5.2% litharge, 2.2% Pb₃O₄). I’m reconverging and repeating the microstructure/inventory checks from this corrected baseline before regenerating the final deliverables.

With corrected litharge, massicot broadening remains supported but is less extreme: the neutral-size fit is Rwp = 14.32% and ~9.8 wt% massicot, while the broadened fit is ~14.02% and ~14.3 wt%. The current domain sizes relax to ~40–46 nm. I’m comparing isotropic and all orthorhombic axes again; if the axis distinction disappears, I’ll simplify to isotropic size.

The corrected baseline simplifies the microstructure conclusion: an isotropic massicot size of ~46 nm reaches Rwp = 14.025%, essentially the same as all three uniaxial models (14.019–14.024%) whose preferred axes are indistinguishable. I’m pruning anisotropy and testing whether strain adds independent information; otherwise the final model will use one isotropic size term.

The corrected inventory is stable: removing litharge worsens Rwp to 15.08%, removing Pb₃O₄ worsens it to 14.22%, and raspite again collapses to zero. Strain hits its upper bound and is rejected. The accepted model is now the simpler isotropic massicot size (~46 nm), with preliminary fractions 67.8% PbWO₄, 10.6% WO₃, 14.1% massicot, 5.2% litharge, and 2.3% Pb₃O₄.

The corrected texture replay confirms no preferred orientation: all tested axes refine to March–Dollase 0.998–1.005 and change Rwp by only ~0.001. The final specification is therefore five phases, no texture, one isotropic massicot size term, and the corrected PbO litharge setting. I’m regenerating and re-auditing all deliverables from that exact project state.

The refinement and export are complete. I’m doing one final consistency check on the submitted project, profile, CIFs, and report before handing them over.

